{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat paper_examples/README.md; cat .neurico/idea.yaml | head -45; cat notes/paper_metadata.json | head -85; cat results/prompts.json; cat results/summary.json'","aggregated_output":"This folder is for storing examples of previous papers, to guide the paper-writer for consistent style, formatting, etc.idea:\n  title: Detect different lies\n  domain: artificial_intelligence\n  hypothesis: 'The literature already proposes several distinctions between types\n    of\n\n    false outputs (e.g., hallucination, confabulation, strategic deception).\n\n    However, current lie-detection methods often evaluate detectors on\n\n    datasets that do not cleanly separate these mechanisms, which makes it\n\n    difficult to know what the detector is actually detecting. When LLMs\n\n    produce false statements, how often are these due to epistemic failure\n\n    versus incentive-driven misreporting?\n\n\n    Concretely: for one model and matched questions, separate false outputs\n\n    where the model does not know the answer (hallucination) from false\n\n    outputs where it knows the answer but misreports it under an incentive\n\n    (lie). What share of false statements falls in each category, and do\n\n    standard white-box lie detectors and hallucination detectors tell the two\n\n    apart, or do they respond to falsehood or uncertainty in general?\n\n    '\n  background:\n    description: \"Why it matters. White-box deception probes are a leading proposal\\\n      \\ for\\nmonitoring models. If a \\\"lie detector\\\" fires on falsehood in general,\\\n      \\ or\\non low-knowledge states, rather than on a mismatch between belief and\\n\\\n      statement, it will flag honest mistakes and may miss confident knowing\\nlies.\\n\\\n      \\nWhat is known. MASK (2503.03750) is a behavioural benchmark that\\nseparates\\\n      \\ honesty (statement vs elicited belief) from accuracy (belief\\nvs ground truth)\\\n      \\ using pressure prompts and neutral belief elicitation,\\nwithout probe analysis\\\n      \\ of the two error types. Liars' Bench (2511.16035)\\ncollects lie and honest\\\n      \\ examples from four open models (24B-72B) over\\nseven settings and finds detectors\\\n      \\ fail on some lie types; its focus is\\nlies vs honest answers, not lies vs\\\n      \\ honest errors. Goldowsky-Dill et al.\\n(2502.03407) train linear deception\\\n      \\ probes on Llama-3.3-70B with high\\nAUROC and note the probes also fire on\\\n[\n  {\n    \"id\": \"2503.03750\",\n    \"title\": [\n      \"The MASK Benchmark: Disentangling Honesty From Accuracy in AI Systems\"\n    ],\n    \"authors\": [\n      \"Ren, Richard\",\n      \"Agarwal, Arunim\",\n      \"Mazeika, Mantas\",\n      \"Menghini, Cristina\",\n      \"Vacareanu, Robert\",\n      \"Kenstler, Brad\",\n      \"Yang, Mick\",\n      \"Barrass, Isabelle\",\n      \"Gatti, Alice\",\n      \"Yin, Xuwang\",\n      \"Trevino, Eduardo\",\n      \"Geralnik, Matias\",\n      \"Khoja, Adam\",\n      \"Lee, Dean\",\n      \"Yue, Summer\",\n      \"Hendrycks, Dan\"\n    ],\n    \"date\": [\n      \"2025/03/05\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n      \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.03750\",\n      \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.03750v3\",\n      \"https://static.arxiv.org/icons/twitter/arxiv-logo-twitter-square.png\",\n      \"https://static.arxiv.org/js/bibex-dev/bibex.css?20200709\",\n      \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.03750\",\n      \"https://arxiv.org/IgnoreMe\",\n      \"https://arxiv.org/\",\n      \"https://arxiv.org/search\",\n      \"https://arxiv.org/user/create\",\n      \"https://info.arxiv.org/about/donate.html\",\n      \"https://arxiv.org/login\",\n      \"https://arxiv.org/search\",\n      \"https://arxiv.org/search/advanced\",\n      \"http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#\",\n      \"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/\",\n      \"http://madskills.com/public/xml/rss/module/trackback/\",